<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madakari Nayaka V</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1779">1779</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Madakari <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> V was the last of the Nayaka rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chitradurga">Chitradurga</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, reigning from 1758. He made shifting alliances with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and others. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> of Mysore besieged his hill fort, which fell in 1779 after long resistance, and Madakari Nayaka died in captivity. He is a folk hero in Karnataka, as is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onake-obavva">Onake Obavva</a>, a woman celebrated for defending the fort.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madakari-nayaka-v.jpg" alt="Madakari Nayaka V" width="238" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nikhil0000711 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madakarinayaka_statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The Chitradurga Nayakas had begun as local governors under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> and became effectively independent after that empire lost control of its heartland in the late 16th century. Their kingdom in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> was organised around Chitradurga Fort, its political and military centre. Madakari, also known as Veera Madakari Nayaka, succeeded Kasturi Rangappa Nayaka II. He is numbered the fifth of his name in the conventional list of the dynasty's rulers, although the numbering of the rulers called Madakari varies between accounts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His reign fell during the struggle between the expanding Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> for central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>. Chitradurga's rulers negotiated with the Marathas in the hope that their support would keep the kingdom independent, while at other times Madakari cooperated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a>. Hyder Ali's forces clashed with Chitradurga repeatedly in the 1760s and 1770s, and the hill fort, with its successive lines of walls, gateways, storehouses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> built into the rocky ground, withstood these earlier attacks.</p><p>The campaign of 1779 ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> rule. After the fort was taken the kingdom became a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, and Madakari and members of his family were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a> as prisoners. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> also moved a large number of Bedar soldiers and their families from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chitradurga">Chitradurga</a> to Srirangapatna, and many of the men were taken into the Mysore army. Chitradurga stayed under Mysore after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> in 1799, when the Wadiyars were restored.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Madakari died in captivity at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> he is remembered as Veera Madakari Nayaka, the last defender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chitradurga">Chitradurga</a>'s independence. In the best-known tradition of the sieges, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onake-obavva">Onake Obavva</a>, the wife of a soldier on guard, found enemy soldiers entering through a narrow gap in the rocks and killed several with a wooden pestle before help came; the gap is called Onake Obavvana Kindi, though accounts differ over which attack it was. The surviving walls, parts of them built under earlier dynasties or under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>, are the chief remains of the Nayaka state. T. R. Subba Rao's novel Durgaasthamaana, which received the Sahitya Akademi Award for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> posthumously in 1985, and B. L. Venu's Gandugali Madakari Nayaka tell his story.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Madakari Nayaka V” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Madakari Nayaka V</b>, the last ruler, is a folk hero in Karnataka.</blockquote><a href="/state/chitradurga-nayakas">Chitradurga Nayakas</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=12.42,76.7&amp;year=1769" aria-label="Srirangapatna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Madakari Nayaka V"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3899 3946h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3899 3946h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=12.42,76.7&amp;year=1769">What was here in 1769? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
